  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 AM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nWeak upslope northeast winds will allow for fog formation this\nmorning at KDDC and KGCK, which may be slow to burn off today \ngiven the moist low levels, cloud cover and upslope winds. An \nupper level low and associated 700-300mb cold pool will move \nnortheastward into south central Kansas today, with temperatures\nin that layer cooling about 5C from today's values. A cold \nfront will move across southwest Kansas late this morning \nthrough late this afternoon, with north winds gusting as high as\n35 mph. T-storms will form along the front and also ahead of \nthe front given the mid to upper level cooling and surface based\nCAPE values of at least 1000 j/kg. Low level shear will be \nabsent today as winds will be light to easterly from the surface\nup through 500mb. Better high level shear may allow for some \npersistent updrafts, with some potential for small hail. In the\nwake of the front, storms will be elevated, with a transition \nto stratiform rain that will taper off from northwest to \nsoutheast. The flood watch was maintained for today despite the \nlack of flooding so far. Given the saturated conditions and \nexpected slow storm movement, additional heavy rains could lead\nto flooding in the watch area. Temperatures won't get above the\n60s for locations that have a frontal passage by mid to late \nmorning, with 70s across south central Kansas. With the strong \nnorth winds, it will feel chilly.\n\nLows are expected to be in the 40s across far western Kansas\ntonight after skies clear, with 40s across most of central and\nwestern Kansas by Saturday morning. A warming trend will\ncommence by the weekend, with highs warming into the 80s by\nearly next week. All of the ensemble means show the mid to high\nlevel westerlies across the northern tier of states, with weak \nupper ridging across the southern Rockies and western high \nplains. The warm pattern will stick around through at least next \nSaturday and possibly into Sunday before the next cold front \narrives.",
